% §5 Experiments. Every number below is traceable to a file under results/; the source is
% given in a comment next to the claim. Regenerate with the commands in README.md.
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We test one prediction: each width responds to exactly one acquisition. $\Wcls$ and $\Wunk$
should contract with the unlabelled log and ignore labels; $\Wlab$ should contract with labels,
widen with label noise, and ignore the log. A single epistemic score cannot have this
structure. \Cref{sec:exp-synthetic} checks the prediction where every component has a known
ground truth. \Cref{sec:exp-real} asks whether the guarantee and the attribution survive frozen
CLIP features on CIFAR-10.

\subsection{Protocol}
\label{sec:exp-protocol}

\paragraph{Response slopes.} For each acquisition we sweep its budget on a log-spaced grid and
hold the other two at a base value ($n=4096$, $m=256$, $\eta=0$ in the synthetic study). The
response of a width is the least-squares slope of $\log(\text{mean width})$ against
$\log(\text{budget})$, so $-1/2$ is the parametric rate. Confidence intervals come from a
bootstrap over seeds. A seed shares its draws across the grid, so it is the independent
replicate; cells and probes are not.

\paragraph{What counts as no response.} A slope is never exactly zero in finite samples, so we
calibrate ``$\approx 0$'' against a placebo instead of choosing a margin. For each cell we
re-evaluate the base budget on fresh, disjoint seeds, assign them to the grid levels at random,
and refit the slope; any slope there is Monte Carlo noise of that estimator at that seed count.
A response is called nonzero when its slope lies outside the $1-\alpha/K$ quantile of the
absolute placebo slopes, with $\alpha=0.05$ and $K=25$ pre-registered cells. The resulting
tolerance $\tau$ is reported per cell and never exceeds $0.043$.
% results/response_l2_s128/summary.json: placebo_alpha_per_cell=0.002, max_tau=0.0429
Placebo levels are unpaired while the real grid is paired by seed, which makes the null wider
than the paired noise: the test is conservative for a signed response and liberal for
$\approx 0$. Some invariances hold by construction, because a width never reads that
acquisition (for instance, $\Wcls$ never reads labels). We mark these $0^{\ast}$ and do not
count them as evidence.

\paragraph{Pre-registration and resolution gate.} The expected sign of every cell was written to
\texttt{train/expectations.py} before the first run; its SHA-256 is recorded in each run
manifest. Before any slope is reported, the pipeline checks that the ball can resolve a $0.4$
shift of mixture weight between the two closest sources, i.e.\ that
$\mathrm{res} = \lVert 0.4(\mu_i-\mu_j)\rVert/(\varepsilon_n+r_{\mathrm{ref}}) > 1$ at the
smallest $n$, and refuses to write results otherwise.

\paragraph{Uncertainty set.} All intervals are computed over the exact MMD ball
(\cref{sec:setup}). Linear-fractional extrema become second-order cone programs after the
Charnes--Cooper change of variables. A coordinatewise outer box gives the same decisions on
synthetic data but is about $30\%$ wider there, and near-vacuous with ten sources.
% box vs l2: results/response_s128 vs results/response_l2_s128, W_cls on_support n=65536: 0.033 vs 0.023

\paragraph{Baselines.} EMQ \citep{saerens2002adjusting} with a multinomial bootstrap of the log
gives percentile intervals for the same estimands as $\Wcls$ and $\Wunk$; it is the
prevalence-only comparison and carries no finite-sample guarantee. A bootstrap ensemble that
resamples both the log and the labels gives one scalar per input: the mutual information
$\mathrm{H}(\bar p)-\overline{\mathrm{H}(p)}$ between members and prediction. It stands in for
the scalar epistemic scores the introduction argues against.

\subsection{Synthetic response matrix}
\label{sec:exp-synthetic}

\paragraph{Worlds.} Three sources over four input cells: two known sources with prevalences
$0.65$ and $0.35$ of the known mass, and a novel source with mass $\delta=0.1$. Clean label
rates are $\theta=(0.2,0.8,0.9)$. Probe cells are labelled \emph{on support} (both known sources
place mass there), \emph{boundary} (known-source overlap), and \emph{off support} (no source
places mass, so the conditional is undefined and the interval is $[0,1]$ by policy). In
\emph{separated} the novel source is outside the convex hull of the known conditionals; in
\emph{confounded} it is their midpoint, inside the hull, where the log cannot identify novelty;
in \emph{absent}, $\delta=0$. Grids are $n\in\{2^{10},\dots,2^{16}\}$,
$m\in\{2^{5},\dots,2^{11}\}$ and $\eta\in\{0.0125,\dots,0.2\}$, with 128 seeds per budget.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Response matrix, \emph{separated} world, on-support probes. Each entry is the
log-log slope of mean width against one acquisition; arrows mark responses outside the placebo
band, and $0^{\ast}$ marks invariance by construction. 95\% bootstrap half-widths are below
$0.03$ for every entry. The three REACH widths each respond to one acquisition (two for
$\Wlab$, which reads labels and their noise); the ensemble score responds to all three.}
\label{tab:synthetic}
\input{tables/synthetic_matrix}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Attribution.} \Cref{tab:synthetic} shows the predicted pattern. $\Wcls$ contracts
with the log at the parametric rate (slope $-0.51$, 95\% CI $[-0.508,-0.503]$) and does not
read labels. $\Wlab$ contracts with labels ($-0.47$) and widens with label noise ($+0.16$),
with no response to the log. $\Wunk$ contracts with the log ($-0.49$) on support, and stays at
$1$ at every $n$ off support.
% results/response_l2_s128/response_matrix.csv
Across all three worlds, 23 of the 25 pre-registered cells agree. Boundary probes give the same
signs at lower absolute width (e.g.\ $\Wcls$ falls from $0.42$ to $0.05$ rather than from $0.19$
to $0.02$).

The ensemble score responds to every acquisition: $-0.03$ in $n$, $-0.82$ in $m$, $+0.44$ in
$\eta$. Its magnitude is dominated by the label channel, so on these probes it is almost
insensitive to the log even though the prevalence width falls by a factor of eight over the same
grid. A practitioner reading the ensemble score would therefore buy labels to reduce an
uncertainty that more unlabelled data would also have reduced, and could not tell the two
apart.

\paragraph{The two failures.} Both disagreements are in the \emph{confounded} world, where we
pre-registered that $\Wunk$ would not respond to $n$. It does respond weakly: the slope is
$-0.09$ on support and $-0.04$ on the boundary, against $-0.49$ in the separated world.
The width itself stays large, falling from $1.0$ to $0.69$ (on support) and $0.86$ (boundary)
at $n=2^{16}$.
% results/response_l2_s128: confounded W_unk width_at_max 0.685 / 0.861
The finite-$n$ slope is the ball shrinking onto the identified set, which is a wide interval
rather than a point. Novelty is not identified there, but ``no response'' was the wrong
prediction; ``a response attenuated five-fold onto a width that does not go to zero'' is the
right one. We report the pre-registered decision unchanged.

\paragraph{Coverage.} The ball contains the true marginal in every seed at every $n$, in all
three worlds (Clopper--Pearson lower bound $0.977$ at nominal $0.975$). Probe intervals for
$\Wcls$ and the total posterior cover the truth in every seed. EMQ+bootstrap is about three
times narrower and close to nominal when the model is identified (coverage $0.92$--$0.96$ in
\emph{separated}). In \emph{confounded}, its coverage falls from $0.73$ at $n=2^{10}$ to $0$ at
$n\ge 2^{15}$: EM picks one point of a non-identified set, and the bootstrap reports the
sampling spread around that point, which shrinks onto the wrong value
(\cref{fig:coverage}, left).
% results/response_l2_s128/summary.json probe_coverage
\Cref{fig:wcls} shows $\Wcls$ against $n$ for both region types, with the resolution gate:
resolution is $2.44$ at the smallest $n$ and rises as $n^{1/2}$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig2_wcls_vs_n.pdf}
\caption{Left: $\Wcls$ (solid) and the EMQ+bootstrap width (dotted) against $n$, on-support
and boundary probes, \emph{separated} world; the dashed line has slope $-1/2$. Right: ball
resolution (\cref{sec:exp-protocol}); the gate requires it to exceed $1$ (dashed) at the
smallest $n$.}
\label{fig:wcls}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Real-shift audit: CIFAR-10 with a frozen CLIP encoder}
\label{sec:exp-real}

\paragraph{Pipeline.} Images pass through a frozen CLIP ViT-B/32 \citep{radford2021clip}; we
fit PCA to 32 dimensions and an equal-prior Gaussian LDA head on a 10{,}000-image development
split of the training set. Nothing is fine-tuned. Input cells are the LDA-predicted class plus
an outlier cell for points whose Mahalanobis distance to every class mean exceeds the
development 99th percentile. The mixture components are the cell distributions of each source,
i.e.\ columns of the head's confusion matrix, estimated from $m$ labelled reference images per
source drawn from the remaining 40{,}000 training images. Under label shift these columns do not
move, which is the frozen-conditionals assumption. The deployment log is drawn from the test
set; the head is 91.2\% accurate on clean test images. Each arm uses 50 seeds, and the expected
sign of every tested cell was registered in \texttt{real/expectations.py} before these runs.
Coverage is judged at the cone solver's accuracy ($10^{-6}$): where an exact extremum is $0$
the solver returns values of order $10^{-9}$.
% results/audit_*_label_shift_held_out/summary.json: meta, expectations_sha256, coverage_tolerance

\paragraph{Estimated components need a joint interval.} With oracle components, $\Wcls$ is a
valid interval for the class posterior. With estimated components it is not: it optimises over
mixture weights with the component estimates plugged in, so reference error enters only through
the ball's radius and never through the target. We therefore report a \emph{joint} interval,
whose extrema range over the ball and over the label-channel interval of each component entry.
For fixed weights the extremes sit at interval endpoints, so each side is still one
linear-fractional program. The joint interval carries the coverage claim on real data; $\Wcls$
and $\Wlab$ remain the attribution. The distinction is visible below: plug-in $\Wcls$ covers
$0.98$ under clean label shift and $0.58$ under the strongest corruption, while the joint
interval covers $1.00$ and $0.93$.

\paragraph{How many sources.} With one source per class, the ball cannot resolve sources whose
deployment mass is below its radius. At $m=2000$ the radius is dominated by the reference term,
about $0.10$ after a union bound over sources, so rare classes are close to vacuous. We report a
four-superclass grouping as the headline (vehicles of air and sea, road vehicles, pets, other
animals), with within-group composition held fixed so deployment remains pure label shift over
sources. The ten-source run is reported alongside it as the limitation it is. Both pass the
resolution gate ($1.14$ and $1.62$ at $n=500$).
% results/audit_{super4,classes}_label_shift_held_out/summary.json ball_resolution_gate

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{CIFAR-10 label shift with Dirichlet(1) prevalences, frozen CLIP ViT-B/32,
$n=16{,}000$, $m=2000$, 50 seeds. Per-probe coverage of the true class posterior and mean width
on known-class cells. Below: response of the joint width to each acquisition, others at base.}
\label{tab:real}
\input{tables/real_audit}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Label shift.} Each seed draws its deployment prevalences from a symmetric
Dirichlet(1) over sources, so prevalence varies across replicates and is known exactly for each.
\Cref{tab:real} gives the coverage result. The joint interval covers the true posterior in every
seed at every $n$, for both groupings. EMQ+bootstrap, run on the same estimated components,
covers $0.54$ at $n=500$ and $0.13$ at $n=16{,}000$ (super4; $0.71$ and $0.17$ with ten sources).
The cause is the one from the confounded synthetic world, reached by a different route:
reference error is ignored, so the bootstrap interval shrinks onto a biased point
(\cref{fig:coverage}, centre).
% results/audit_*_label_shift_held_out/summary.json n_axis_coverage, arm=label_shift

\paragraph{Attribution.} All 13 pre-registered cells agree for super4, and 12 of 13 with ten
sources. Within the label channel the pattern matches the synthetic study: $\Wlab$ does not
read the log, contracts with labels (slope $-0.31$) and widens with label noise ($+0.21$). The
joint width responds to all three, about $1.6$ times more strongly to labels than to the log
($-0.11$ in $m$ against $-0.07$ in $n$), so beyond a few thousand log images labels are the
binding acquisition. On real data $m$ and $\eta$ also move $\Wcls$ ($-0.19$ and $+0.12$),
because labels estimate the components the ball is built from; we registered these
cross-effects as signed in advance rather than as zero. EMQ+bootstrap does not respond to labels
or noise at all (both slopes inside the placebo band), and the ensemble score responds to $n$
and $m$ but not to $\eta$, because its members resample the debiased components and never see
the noise level. The one disagreement is the ten-source joint width under noise: it is already
at $0.99$ at $\eta=0.025$ and cannot widen further, so its slope is inside the placebo band.
% results/audit_super4_label_shift_held_out/response_matrix.csv on_support; classes summary.json disagreeing

\paragraph{Label noise.} Each reference label is flipped with known probability $\eta$ to a
uniformly chosen other source. The flip matrix $M$ is known and invertible for
$\eta<(k-1)/k$, so the components are recovered as $M^{-1}$ times the noisy-label histograms
and projected onto the simplex. Their radius has two parts: cell sampling given which images
landed under each noisy label, and the deviation of the realised flip counts from their means,
which vanishes as $\sqrt{\eta}$; $M^{-1}$ inflates both by at most $\lVert M^{-1}\rVert_\infty$.
Over $\eta\in\{0.025,\dots,0.3\}$ the joint interval covers in every seed (super4: width $0.92$
to $1.00$; $\Wlab$ $0.59$ to $0.97$). EMQ+bootstrap covers between $0.11$ and $0.18$.
% results/audit_super4_label_shift_held_out/summary.json eta_axis_coverage

\paragraph{Held-out classes, present and absent.} We remove ship and truck from the labelled set
and the head; their distance to the hull of the known components is $s=0.22$. In one arm they
make up $\delta=0.1$ of the log, in the other they are absent ($\delta=0$). The novel component
is estimated from the withheld classes' training images, so, as for $\Wcls$, the plug-in $\Wunk$
is the attribution and a joint novelty interval, which also ranges over the novel and known
component entries (\cref{sec:theory}, Remark), carries coverage. The joint novelty interval covers
the truth in every seed, in both arms, for both backbones and both groupings. Plug-in $\Wunk$
does not: with DINOv2 features and novelty present it covers only $0.88$--$0.89$. Both widths
contract in both arms. With CLIP and super4 sources, plug-in $\Wunk$ falls from $0.70$ to $0.43$
with novelty present and from $0.64$ to $0.37$ with novelty absent, where the true novel
posterior is $0$; the joint widths fall from $0.92$ to $0.72$ and from $0.89$ to $0.66$. Both arms
are on support in the sense of \cref{prop:c}: the known classes keep every cell populated, so
the contraction is the predicted one. The off-support case, a width that never shrinks, is shown
only in the synthetic study, where it holds by the vacuous policy. With a declared uniform novel
component the coverage still holds, $\Wunk$ barely moves ($0.97\to0.86$), and our residual
diagnostic never flags the misspecification (rate $0$ in all runs). We therefore make no claim
that the audit detects a wrong novelty model.
% results/audit_{clip,dinov2}_{super4,classes}_held_out_v2/summary.json and response_matrix.csv

\paragraph{Covariate shift.} CIFAR-10-C \citep{hendrycks2019benchmarking} breaks the
frozen-conditionals assumption: the label set stays clean and only the log sees the corruption.
Head accuracy falls from $0.83$ at severity 1 to $0.57$ at severity 5. Over 15 corruptions,
20 seeds each, with super4 sources, the incompatibility flag rises with severity from $0.06$ to
$0.55$. The joint interval covers at least $0.987$ through severity 4 and $0.93$ at severity 5,
while plug-in $\Wcls$ falls from $0.95$ to $0.58$ and EMQ+bootstrap from $0.12$ to $0.05$
(\cref{fig:coverage}, right). Pooling hides failures: at severity 5, elastic transform ($0.74$),
motion blur ($0.85$), JPEG ($0.87$) and frost ($0.90$) under-cover while flagged in fewer than half
their runs. With ten sources the flag fires in at most $6\%$ of runs and coverage at severity 5
is $0.91$, with ten corruptions under-covering unflagged.
% results/audit_{super4,classes}_corruption/summary.json (severity, severity_by_corruption); run on Modal

\paragraph{A second backbone.} To check that nothing above is specific to CLIP, we repeat the
label-shift and held-out arms with a self-supervised DINOv2 ViT-S/14 encoder \citep{oquab2024dinov2},
which sees no text. Its head is more accurate ($95.2\%$ against $91.2\%$) and passes the resolution
gate ($1.22$ with super4 sources, $1.75$ with ten). The pre-registered cells agree as before: 13 of
13 with super4 sources and 12 of 13 with ten, where the same saturated noise cell is the one
disagreement. The joint interval covers in every seed in every arm; at $n=16{,}000$ its width is
$0.69$ (super4; $0.86$ with ten sources) and EMQ+bootstrap covers $0.10$ ($0.23$). The response
pattern of the joint width is the same as with CLIP: $-0.07$ in $n$, $-0.12$ in $m$, $+0.04$ in
$\eta$. We did not repeat the corruption arm for DINOv2.
% results/audit_dinov2_{super4,classes}_label_shift_held_out/summary.json, response_matrix.csv

\paragraph{Where \cref{prop:c} stops: a novel class beside its siblings.} On CIFAR-100 we take
four trees (maple, oak, palm, pine) and five vehicles as known, grouped into two sources, and add
either willow, a sibling of the known trees, or whale, unrelated to every known class, as the novel
source with $\delta=0.1$. We registered that willow should give a smaller separation $s$, a flatter
$\Wunk$ slope, and a wider final $\Wunk$ than whale, not a low $\Wunk$. With 400 reference images
per class the novel component's radius alone is about $0.21$, and the resolution gate passes only
for $n\ge 8{,}000$; we therefore report this arm descriptively. With CLIP features all three
predictions hold: $s=0.30$ against $0.46$, slope $-0.02$ (inside the placebo band) against $-0.09$,
and final width $0.57$ against $0.51$. With DINOv2 features only the slope prediction holds
($-0.03$ against $-0.06$): under that encoder's partition willow is \emph{further} from the known
hull than whale ($s=0.35$ against $0.32$), and the final widths reverse accordingly. The joint
novelty interval covers in all four runs. The lesson for \cref{prop:c} is that the relevant
quantity is $s$ measured in the encoder's cell space, not semantic similarity: whether a sibling
class is ``inside the support'' depends on the representation.
% results/audit_c100g_{in,out}_{clip,dinov2}/summary.json; gate fails for n < 8000 (min 0.66-0.69 at n=500).
% Deviation from registration (real/expectations.py): known classes grouped into two sources after
% singleton sources failed the gate at every n; disclose in the appendix.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig3_coverage.pdf}
\caption{Per-probe coverage of REACH and EMQ+bootstrap. Left: synthetic; the REACH curves for
both worlds lie on $1.0$. Centre: CIFAR-10 label shift with Dirichlet(1) prevalences, four
superclass sources. Right: CIFAR-10-C pooled over 15 corruptions, with the rate at which the
mixture-incompatibility diagnostic fires. Dashed: nominal $0.95$.}
\label{fig:coverage}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Shifts nobody constructed: does the regime choose the action?}
\label{sec:exp-blindspot}

On natural and corrupted shifts there is no ground-truth width, so the test is behavioural: take
the acquisition REACH recommends at each cell and measure what it actually buys on held-out
shifted data, against the same check for a scalar epistemic score. Protocol, budget and claims
were registered before the test was written (\texttt{real/blindspot\_prereg.py}).

\paragraph{Design.} Shift splits are clean CIFAR-10 test, CIFAR-10.1 \citep{recht2019imagenet}, a
natural re-collection, and the 75 CIFAR-10-C splits; each is cut once into disjoint log, label
and evaluation pools. An episode draws deployment prevalences from Dirichlet(1) over the four
superclass sources and starts from clean components ($m=2000$) and a log of $1000$ shifted
images. Two actions cost the same $5000$ units at a price of $50$ unlabelled images per label:
\emph{reweight} grows the log to $6000$ and refits EMQ; \emph{add labels} buys $100$ shifted labels
and shrinks the components towards them (prior strength $50$). The payoff of an action at a
cell is the reduction in held-out negative log-likelihood there. REACH's regime uses only the
starting state: \emph{off-frame} if the mixture is flagged incompatible or the cell is the outlier
cell, otherwise \emph{log resolves} or \emph{label-limited} by which action shrinks the joint width
more at that cost. Off-frame and label-limited recommend labels. The baseline is ensemble mutual
information, whose members resample both log and labels; its tercile-to-action map is tuned on
calibration seeds 0--9, which favours it, and both are evaluated on seeds 10--19.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Realised payoff (reduction in held-out NLL, nats) of each action, by ensemble-MI
tercile and REACH regime, mass-weighted over cells; last column: share of cells where labels
pay more. 75 CIFAR-10-C splits, clean CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-10.1, evaluation seeds.}
\label{tab:blindspot}
\input{tables/blindspot}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Results.} All three registered claims hold. Within a fixed MI tercile, the advantage
of labels over reweighting is larger where REACH recommends labels: by $0.83$ nats (95\% CI
$[0.69, 0.97]$) in the lowest tercile and $0.57$ $[0.47, 0.66]$ in the middle one; in the highest
tercile the difference is $0.07$ $[-0.01, 0.14]$. The blind spot is the first block of
\cref{tab:blindspot}: cells where ensemble MI is lowest, and its tuned map therefore reweights,
but REACH flags the frame as broken, are where labels pay most ($1.20$ nats, best in $88\%$ of
cells). REACH's recommendation has lower regret than the tuned baseline map by $0.085$ nats per
cell $[0.060, 0.111]$ and matches the better action more often ($0.62$ against $0.57$). The
advantage of labels rises monotonically with corruption severity (Spearman $1.0$), as it should
when the components move.
% results/blindspot/summary.json: H1, H2, H3; prereg_sha256 e699e72d...

\paragraph{What the natural shift adds, and what it does not.} Almost all of this effect comes
from CIFAR-10-C. On CIFAR-10.1 the head loses seven points of accuracy ($84.5\%$ against
$91.2\%$), yet REACH never flags the mixture as incompatible and neither action buys much: the
better action gains $0.008$ nats on average, and $100$ shifted labels slightly hurt
($-0.010$). REACH's lower regret there ($0.008$ $[0.005, 0.012]$) comes from not spending on
labels where the tuned baseline does. So on this natural shift the two scores disagree but the
stakes are small, and the honest reading is that CIFAR-10.1 is not a shift where the choice of
acquisition matters for this head. The regime earns its keep where the components move.
% results/blindspot/rows.csv.gz, split=cifar10_1 / clean / severity>0, evaluation seeds; regret
% differences by family: cifar10_1 -0.0083 [-0.0116,-0.0053], clean -0.0024, C10C -0.0868.

\paragraph{Not run.} CIFAR-10H, ImageNet-R/Sketch, DomainNet, and the corruption arm for DINOv2 were not run.
